  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 213 AM CDT Tue Aug 25 2026\n\nPrecipitation Trends...Periods of showers and thunderstorms are \nanticipated from late this morning through Wednesday evening. For \nlate this morning into this afternoon, a weak 850mb jet and \nincreasing WAA will drive the potential for isolated showers and \nstorms (25-35%) mainly across the Northwoods. Later this evening \nthrough late tonight, chances for showers and storms increase to 40-\n60% spreading across the entire forecast area as a cold front sweeps \nacross the area. During the late evening (after 9 PM), there appears \nto be a small window for a few stronger storms to move into north-\ncentral and central WI. Surface instability and deep-layer shear \nwill be greatest northwest of the area and will be waning as the \nstorms move eastward into the forecast area. However, cannot rule \nout the potential for the ongoing convection to produce damaging \nwind gusts and hail in central and north-central WI before \nweakening. The strong/severe storm potential will decrease a few \nhours after 9 PM as the storms become elevated. Additional showers \nand non-severe storms are possible Wednesday, mainly during the \nafternoon, as the surface cold front's mid-level shortwave trough \nmoves overhead. By Wednesday evening, probabilities for 0.50 inch or \nmore of precip range between roughly 20-40% mainly across the \nNorthwoods and Door County, with less than 10% elsewhere, but would \nstill expect a few localized areas to receive closer to an inch of \nrain under the stronger convection.\n\nAfter Wednesday, dry weather is anticipated for the remainder of the \nwork week with the next chances for showers and storms arriving \nsometime this weekend. Will need to monitor for the severe weather \npotential as the pattern may favor this solution on one of the days \nsomewhere across the Midwest, but there are timing and placement \ndifferences that will need to be resolved until then.\n\nTemperatures...Throughout the week and into the weekend, \ntemperatures, both highs and lows, will hover around normal. Highs \nwill mainly be in the 70s to low 80s, with lows generally in the 50s \nto low 60s, but locations across the Northwoods could see lows in \nthe 40s Thursday night into Friday morning.",
